Regulations on the Administration of Online Publishing Services | Art. 9 | Chapter II Service Licensing for Online Publishing :: Chapter II Service Licensing for Online Publishing

Article 7: Any entity engaged in online publishing services must obtain approval from the publishing administrative authorities according to law and obtain a "Network Publishing Service License."

Article 8: Publishing units of books, audiovisual materials, electronic publications, newspapers, and periodicals engaged in online publishing services shall possess the following conditions:
(1) A definite website domain name, intelligent terminal application programs, or other publishing platforms through which to conduct online publishing business;
(2) A definite service scope for online publishing;
(3) The necessary technical equipment required for conducting online publishing services; the relevant servers and storage equipment must be located within the territory of the People's Republic of China.

Article 9: Other entities engaged in online publishing services shall, in addition to the conditions listed in Article 8, also possess the following conditions:
(1) A definite and non-overlapping name and articles of association of the entity conducting online publishing services, which must not duplicate those of other publishing units;
(2) A legal representative and principal responsible persons conforming to state regulations; the legal representative must be a Chinese citizen with full legal capacity who resides permanently within Chinese territory; at least one of the legal representative and principal responsible persons shall possess a professional qualification in publishing at or above the intermediate level;
(3) In addition to the legal representative and principal responsible persons, at least 8 full-time editorial and publishing personnel with professional qualifications in publishing and related professions recognized by the National Press and Publication Administration, of whom no fewer than 3 shall hold qualifications at or above the intermediate level, to meet the needs of the scope of online publishing services;
(4) A content review and proofreading system required for conducting online publishing services;
(5) A fixed place of business;
(6) Other conditions as prescribed by laws, administrative regulations, and the National Press and Publication Administration.

Article 10: Units with Chinese-foreign joint ventures, Chinese-foreign cooperative ventures, and wholly foreign-owned operations shall not engage in online publishing services.
Units engaged in online publishing services that cooperate with domestically-registered Chinese-foreign joint ventures, Chinese-foreign cooperative ventures, wholly foreign-owned enterprises, or overseas organizations and individuals in online publishing service projects shall report in advance to the National Press and Publication Administration for approval.

Article 11: Those applying to engage in online publishing services shall submit an application to the publishing administrative authorities of the province, autonomous region, or municipality where they are located; after review and approval, the application shall be reported to the National Press and Publication Administration for approval. The National Press and Publication Administration shall, within 60 days of receiving the application...

**Personal Information Protection Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 55**

Chapter Five: Obligations of Personal Information Processors; Section Three: Special Provisions on Processing Personal Information by State Organs

[Note: The provided text appears to contain Articles 53-57, with Article 55 being the primary target]

**Article 53** — Foreign personal information processors as referred to in the second paragraph of Article 3 of this Law shall establish a specialized institution or designate a representative within the territory of the People's Republic of China to be responsible for handling matters related to personal information protection, and shall submit the name or name and contact information of such institution or representative to the department performing personal information protection responsibilities.

**Article 54** — Personal information processors shall periodically conduct compliance audits on their compliance with laws and administrative regulations in processing personal information.

**Article 55** — In any of the following circumstances, a personal information processor shall conduct a personal information protection impact assessment in advance and make records of the processing situation:
(1) Processing sensitive personal information;
(2) Utilizing personal information for automated decision-making;
(3) Entrusting the processing of personal information, providing personal information to other personal information processors, or disclosing personal information;
(4) Providing personal information to foreign countries;
(5) Other personal information processing activities that have significant impact on personal rights and interests.

**Article 56** — The personal information protection impact assessment shall include the following content:
(1) Whether the purposes and methods of processing personal information are lawful, fair, and necessary;
(2) The impact on personal rights and interests and security risks;
(3) Whether the protective measures adopted are lawful, effective, and commensurate with the degree of risk.

The personal information protection impact assessment report and processing situation records shall be retained for at least three years.

**Article 57** — In the event that personal information leakage, tampering, or loss occurs or may occur, the personal information processor shall immediately adopt remedial measures and notify the department performing personal information protection responsibilities and the individual. [Text incomplete in source]
